fix(pip): Give the scope's TAA skip mask somewhere to land

Turning DLSS off changed nothing, and that is the clue that closes this: with the upscaler off the
SSS TAA takes over (phase_ssfx_taa is gated on !dlss::Configured()), so both temporal filters were
doing the same thing to the sight's pixels all along - reprojecting a screen locked image with the
scene's motion vectors and dragging the previous frame across it while panning.

The pack's shader already writes what is needed. At the end of models_scope_reticle.ps:

    // z keeps the existing HUD classification; w is the SSFX TAA skip mask.
    // The PiP image is already spatially antialiased in its own viewport and
    // must not be accumulated again through the main camera's temporal history.
    result.taa_mask = float4(0, 0, 0, 1);

and ssfx_taa.ps reads that mask from the motion vector target's alpha:

    float vel_acc = s_motion_vectors.SampleLevel(smp_point, I.tc0.xy, 0).a;

The pass that draws the optic bound only two targets (rt_Generic_0, rt_Position), so SV_Target2 had
nowhere to go: the mask was written into nothing and the sight's pixels kept the world's motion
vectors. Binding the motion vector target as the third slot lands both halves of the fix at once -
the mask in the alpha, which tells the filter to keep the current frame, and zero in the RGB, which
is the correct velocity for something that does not move with the scene.

This applies to the pass render_Reticle() runs in, which with r__3Dfakescope on (the pack's default
here) is where the sight's screen is drawn. The same binding for the non fake scope path is a
separate, smaller exercise; it is named here rather than guessed at.